WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:13.074
I'm Darren George, and I grow
and restore bonsai trees.

00:00:13.074 --> 00:00:14.550
I'm going to walk you through

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.400
how I restore an overgrown bonsai tree.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.180
The tree that I'm working on
today is called cotoneaster.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.271
This particular tree was started

00:00:23.271 --> 00:00:26.587
by a bonsai professional
up north in England

00:00:26.587 --> 00:00:27.840
and came into my possession

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.900
when the previous owner
decided to give up bonsai

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.390
and had let the tree deteriorate somewhat.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.850
The tree has fallen into disrepair.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.430
It has several overgrown branches,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.140
and there are also a number of branches

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.733
that have completely died.

00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:43.850
The deadwood is rotting

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.033
and falling away from the tree.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.898
The first step in
restoring this bonsai tree

00:00:52.898 --> 00:00:55.830
is going to be cleaning
out the massive branches

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.680
so that I can see the underlying
structure of the tree.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.950
I'm going to remove any dead pieces

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.810
and generally thin out the
massive leaves and twigs.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.430
Now I can see what's going
on inside of the canopy.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.280
I can see that there
are a couple of branches

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.530
that are growing from the same position.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.060
They're crowding each other out

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.210
and making the tree look quite messy

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.460
and spoiling the flow of the design.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.040
So I'm going to select one or two branches

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.100
to completely remove to open up some space

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.040
to ensure the health of
the branches that remain,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.465
and also create a more open

00:01:34.465 --> 00:01:37.248
and rounded feel to the canopy.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.570
Where I am removing a larger branch,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.990
I'm going to use a special
putty that we call Cut Paste.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.345
The Cut Paste helps the wound heal

00:01:48.345 --> 00:01:50.220
much faster than it ordinarily would

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.673
and prevents rot and bacteria setting in.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.030
Where I've created some
space in the canopy,

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.517
I'm going to use some wire to reposition

00:02:00.517 --> 00:02:02.700
a couple of the remaining branches,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.575
so that we've got quite a nice,

00:02:04.575 --> 00:02:06.900
even distribution of
foliage around the tree,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.920
and remove any large gaps in the canopy.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.720
And once they're all in position,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.300
the work for this moment is complete,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.960
and I can allow the tree to respond

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.749
and grow into the new shape.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.400
Several months later in the spring,

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.357
and the state of the deadwood

00:02:28.357 --> 00:02:30.210
is really starting to bother me.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.176
The longer I leave it,

00:02:31.176 --> 00:02:33.009
the more it's going to deteriorate,

00:02:33.009 --> 00:02:35.820
so I'd like to get the
deadwood treated and hardened.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.850
Before I apply the
treatment to the deadwood,

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.730
I have to ensure that the
deadwood is thoroughly dry.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.790
I keep it out of the elements,

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.490
under shelter, so that it can dry out.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.850
I'm using a two-part epoxy resin.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.050
It has a solvent mixed in with it

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.725
that encourages the resin

00:02:54.725 --> 00:02:57.960
to penetrate the soft deadwood

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.158
so that the epoxy can harden

00:03:01.158 --> 00:03:02.716
further into the core of the wood

00:03:02.716 --> 00:03:05.991
and hopefully allow it to last longer.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.810
I'm applying the epoxy
resin to the deadwood

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.035
as carefully as I can, trying to ensure

00:03:12.035 --> 00:03:15.776
not to get it on the
live wood or on the soil.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.988
I'm going to let it dry
for a couple of days.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.570
Now that the epoxy is completely dry,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.130
I'm rigorously brushing it

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.610
to take the sheen off
the surface of the wood.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.820
I really want it to look
old and sun-bleached

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.950
rather than shiny and new.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.480
So using a steel brush,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.950
it roughs up the surface

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.792
and takes that reflection off the wood.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.750
After a long and hot summer,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.900
this tree has absolutely
exploded with growth.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.840
The branches are super long, super thick,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.760
and really, really healthy.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.590
Once again, I need to come along,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.990
I need to cut back these branches

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.810
to regain the shape of the canopy.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.640
I'm also checking for any opportunities

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.592
to reposition any branches

00:04:07.592 --> 00:04:10.560
that aren't in the ideal locations.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.780
So with all these really long shoots

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:14.310
that have grown over summer,

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:18.210
I'm going to trim them back
to regain the silhouette.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.490
And it also gives me another opportunity

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.127
to apply some wire to reposition

00:04:23.127 --> 00:04:25.290
any new branches that have developed since

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.083
and continue filling in the tree's crown.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.210
Now, with all the watering
that I've been doing

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.380
over the hot summer,

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.290
we've got some hard-water stains built up

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.840
around the base of the tree,
on the bottom of the trunk,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.050
and on some of the roots
on the soil surface.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.533
I'm using a toothbrush and vinegar

00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:47.982
to try and dissolve and scrub off

00:04:47.982 --> 00:04:50.987
some of that white calcium buildup.

00:04:58.982 --> 00:04:59.952
On the soil surface,

00:04:59.952 --> 00:05:02.910
there are some undissolved
fertilizer pellets.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.290
I'll quickly brush those off

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.543
to reveal the green moss underneath.

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.000
And that concludes the work on this tree.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.786
Now I'm done with this bonsai.

00:05:15.786 --> 00:05:18.870
I'm really excited to see how it responds

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.095
and continue working with it

00:05:21.095 --> 00:05:23.478
as it grows into the future.

